# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: autocad-output-explicit-nil-file-rejected -- AutoCAD 2022 rejects (princ X nil), the spec says nil means the command line
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: dialect-divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-printer-explicit-nil-file
#+DATE: 2026-11-04

* Observed

Found while fixing alfe-autocad-fixed-arity-output-shadows (alfe 2.3.87), on
pfenny (Windows), AutoCAD 2022 accoreconsole (ACADVER 24.1, LISPSYS 0), with
the NATIVE primitives (alfe no longer redefines them there):

#+begin_example
alfe -norc --autocad --mode batch \
  -x "(foreach f (quote (princ print prin1))
        (setq r (vl-catch-all-apply f (list \"x\" nil)))
        (princ (strcat \"\nNILFILE \" (vl-symbol-name f) \" \"
                       (if (vl-catch-all-error-p r) (vl-catch-all-error-message r)
                           (vl-prin1-to-string r)))))" --quit
NILFILE PRINC type d'argument incorrect: FILE nil
NILFILE PRINT type d'argument incorrect: FILE nil
NILFILE PRIN1 type d'argument incorrect: FILE nil
#+end_example

So on AutoCAD 2022 an explicit NIL file argument is an error ("bad argument
type: FILE nil"), for all three. The 1-argument and file-descriptor forms work.

* The divergence

The AutoLISP Spec's princ / print / prin1 entries say of =fileHandle=: "If
omitted or =nil=, output goes to the command line." clautolisp follows that
(an explicit nil is accepted in every dialect). AutoCAD 2022 does not.

Open questions (record per AGENTS.md "Unverified Spec Behaviour" until
probed):

1. What do Autodesk's and Bricsys's reference pages say about an explicit
   nil (is "or nil" in the spec ours, or theirs)?
2. BricsCAD V25 / V26 behaviour (expected to accept it, untested).
3. AutoCAD at LISPSYS 1 / 2, and AutoCAD 2026.
4. Classification (AGENTS.md, Dialect Divergence): case 2 or 3, and what
   =--autocad= must do (reproduce the error and warn?).

* Also relevant to alfe

Code alfe evaluates itself on AutoCAD has its output calls rewritten onto
=autolisp-princ-to= & co., which accept a nil FILE. So =(princ "x" nil)= works
in an =-l= file under alfe and fails in the same code compiled into a VLX.
Documented in the alfe user manual (Output on AutoCAD); whether alfe should
reproduce AutoCAD's error there follows from the classification above.

* Resolved (clautolisp / alfe 2.3.88, 2026-11-04)

Answers to the open questions:

1. Both references document an explicit NIL. Autodesk's princ page types
   =file-desc= "File or nil"; Bricsys's says "if omitted or NIL is used,
   output goes to command line". So the spec's "or nil" is the vendors'.
2. BricsCAD V26 (macOS, launched directly with =-B= so alfe's variadic
   shadows were not in the way) accepts it: =(princ "x" nil)=,
   =(print "x" nil)= and =(prin1 "x" nil)= return ="x"=; so do
   =(write-line "x" nil)= and =(write-char 65 nil)= (LISPSYS 0).
3. Not measured: AutoCAD 2026, and AutoCAD 2022 at LISPSYS 1 / 2. pfenny went
   offline mid-probe, and AutoCAD for Mac's accoreconsole has a known headless
   blocker. Recorded as SPEC-UNCERTAIN in %resolved-printer-nil-file-policy and
   under deferred-spec-research.issue; probes/sources/probe-printer.lsp now
   carries the cases.
4. Classification: case 2 (resolved). The references agree and AutoCAD 2022's
   product contradicts its own reference, so the documented behaviour (output
   to the command line) is normative.

What shipped:

- clautolisp: princ / print / prin1 with an explicit NIL file signal the
  bad-argument error under every =--dialect autocad= version (as AutoCAD 2022
  does), print to the command line elsewhere. The =[printer-nil-file]= warning
  fires once per run under the AutoCAD dialects and =--strict=. The
  one-argument call is unchanged everywhere. Test:
  =builtin-printers-explicit-nil-file-per-dialect=.
- alfe: on AutoCAD, a call written with its FILE argument is rewritten onto
  =autolisp-princ-to-file= / =-print-to-file= / =-prin1-to-file=. These hand a
  NIL file to the CAD's own primitive, so alfe-evaluated code fails exactly
  like the same code compiled into a VLX (in 2.3.87 it was silently accepted).
  The alfe tests that passed NIL only to dodge the old 2-argument shadows now
  use the 1-argument form.
- Docs: the AutoLISP Spec 0.14.8 (PRINC "Tested Behaviour (explicit NIL
  file)" and clautolisp notes on PRINC / PRINT / PRIN1); the regenerated
  dialect-warnings annex; the clautolisp and alfe user manuals; RELEASE_NOTES.

Verified on AutoCAD 2022 (pfenny, accoreconsole, 2026-11-05) with alfe 2.3.89
built from master: an alfe-evaluated (princ "x" nil) and (print "y" f) with f
nil now fail with AutoCAD's own "type d'argument incorrect: FILE nil" (exit 1),
as the same code compiled into a VLX does. (princ / prin1 / print X F) on an
open descriptor still write the file ("file-ok\"q\"\r\n3 "). The natively
loaded functions of alfe-autocad-fixed-arity-output-shadows still return DONE /
DONE-ALL, and alfe's own output keeps its bytes (XY\r\n1 "Z"\r\n\r\n).
